<BaseLayout
  title="About · Adam Robertson"
  description="How I lead, how I work, and where I've done it. Product design leader with technical roots, based in Colorado."
>
  <div class="page-rails signal-page">
  {/* Hero -------------------------------------------------------------- */}
  {/*
    Two-column hero rather than a full-bleed statement. The h1 used to run at
    `--text-display` (up to 8.25rem) across three lines, which ate the viewport
    and left the lead paragraph looking like a footnote. Sized down and set
    beside its lead, the two read as one thought — and the emphasis lands on the
    phrase that carries the argument rather than on sheer scale.
  */}
  {/* No `data-blueprint` here: the hero's caption sits at the same height as
      the nav's own "NAVBAR" label and the two collide. */}
  {/* Phones: no min-height to centre within, so the space is set outright —
      the nav's measured height plus the same gap above as below. From md the
      hero keeps its centred 62svh. */}
  <section class="relative flex flex-col overflow-hidden px-shell pb-16 pt-[calc(var(--nav-h,4.3125rem)+4rem)] md:min-h-[62svh] md:pb-[5vh] md:pt-36">
    <NoiseGradient intensity="soft" />
    <div class="hero-crt" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="relative z-10 my-auto w-full">
      {/*
        Centred, a statement rather than a split — deliberately unlike
        /how-i-work's left-aligned, asymmetric hero, so the two pages read as
        two kinds of page (a point of view; a working document) rather than
        one template with different words in it. One line for the headline
        from `md` up, the lead in a narrow measure under it.
      */}
      <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-4xl flex-col items-center gap-y-10 text-center">
        <h1
          class="font-display text-[clamp(2rem,4.3vw,3.75rem)] leading-[1.2] tracking-[-0.02em] text-balance text-ink"
          data-anim="reveal-heading"
        >
          {/* `.phrase-gradient`, not `.accent-gradient`. The latter clips a
              ramp to each element's own box, and `reveal-heading` splits this
              into one element per word, so it would restart on every word and
              read as a rainbow. This one is painted as a single ramp along the
              phrase in reading order — see `paintPhrase` in effects.ts.
              Deliberately no full stop. */}
          I make the <span class="outage outage--b" data-text="room"><span class="outage__tear" data-text="room">room</span></span> <span class="phrase-gradient">better</span>
        </h1>

        <p
          class="max-w-2xl text-[clamp(1.0625rem,1.35vw,1.3rem)] leading-[1.55] text-balance text-ink-lead"
          data-anim="reveal"
        >
          Twenty years in, the most important thing I've learned is that the best design leaders
          aren't the best designers in the room. They're the ones who make the room better. I've
          done that by staying close to the work, staying honest about what's broken, and being
          willing to change how we work when the old way stops serving us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Point of view ------------------------------------------------------ */}
  <section
    class="relative border-t border-line px-shell py-section"
    aria-labelledby="pov"
    data-blueprint="Point of view"
  >
    <RuleCross />
    {/* No centred `max-w-6xl` here. Every other block on the page hangs off
        the `px-shell` edge the hero and the nav wordmark share; boxing this one
        into a centred column indented the heading ~30px off that line at 1440,
        and further on wider screens. The prose caps its own measure. */}
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,18rem)_1fr] lg:gap-20">
      {/* Matched to the home résumé's sticky intro heading rather than
          `text-title` — this is a column label beside body copy, not a section
          banner, and at title scale it overpowered the prose it introduces. */}
      <h2
        id="pov"
        data-text="Point of view"
        style="--signal-dur: 14.9s; --signal-delay: 5s"
        class="signal font-display text-[clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.75rem)] leading-[1.12] text-ink lg:sticky lg:top-[var(--sticky-top)] lg:transition-[top] lg:duration-500 lg:ease-[var(--ease-out-expo)] lg:self-start"
        data-anim="reveal-heading"
      >
        Point of view
      </h2>

      <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink-dim" data-anim="reveal-batch">
        {/* First visual on the page, and it opens the section — the run from
            hero to principles was otherwise 1,500px of unbroken prose. Full
            width of the text column: at 2.36:1 a portrait-sized crop would
            leave the subject too small to read. `.signal-photo` (signal.css)
            puts it on the same tube as the type: scanlines, a slow roll, and
